>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `add_looper`

``` hol4
simpLib.add_looper :
  string * (simpset -> tactic) -> simpset -> simpset
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Adds a named tactic looper to a simpset.

A call to `add_looper (name, make_tac) ss` returns a new simpset.  The
function `make_tac` is given the active simpset and must return a tactic.
The name identifies the looper for updating, deletion, pretty-printing,
and per-invocation exclusion.

Loopers form the tactic layer of simplification.  On a goal, the
simplifier first rewrites, then tries the selected final solvers.  If no
solver closes the goal, it tries the registered loopers in list order,
using the first one that succeeds.  After a looper succeeds, the whole
sequence is restarted independently on every subgoal it produced.  A
looper may also close the goal.

A looper must fail when it is not applicable.  In particular, it should
not behave like `ALL_TAC`: success without progress causes the restart
to repeat indefinitely when the simpset is unlimited.  Raising
`Conv.UNCHANGED` is treated as tactic failure.  A successful application
should close the goal or make genuine progress.

Looper names are unique within a simpset.  If `name` is new,
`add_looper` appends it, so older registrations have priority.  If the
name is already present, its function is replaced in its existing
position.  Thus updating a looper does not change the order in which
loopers are tried.

The bound installed by `simpLib.limit n` also bounds looper execution.
There are at most `n` successful looper rounds in one top-level
simplification-tactic invocation, with one counter shared by all
subgoals.  Failed looper attempts do not consume the counter.  An
unlimited simpset has no such protection against a non-progressing
looper.

In a theorem-list argument, `Excl name` suppresses the named looper for
that simplifier invocation without changing the original simpset.  Use
`del_looper` for persistent removal from a derived simpset.

Loopers are used by tactic simplifiers based on `GEN_SIMP_TAC`, including
`SIMP_TAC` and `ASM_SIMP_TAC`.  They are deliberately outside the
conversion boundary: `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, `SIMP_RULE`, and
`ASM_SIMP_RULE` do not run them.  The rule-based phases of compound
tactics likewise remain conversion-only.

### Failure

`add_looper` itself never fails and does not run the supplied function.
A later tactic invocation may fail internally or diverge if the looper
violates the contract above.

### Example

This simpset splits conjunction goals after ordinary simplification has
reached a fixed point:

``` hol4
fun split_conjunctions _ = CONJ_TAC

val conjunction_ss =
  simpLib.empty_ss
  |> simpLib.add_looper ("split conjunctions", split_conjunctions)

(* SIMP_TAC conjunction_ss [] turns the goal p /\ q into goals p and q. *)

(* This invocation leaves a conjunction goal unsplit. *)
(* SIMP_TAC conjunction_ss [Excl "split conjunctions"] *)
```

